{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>Take for yourself seven and seven.</b> Rashi explained that the reason He commanded to bring seven of the clean animals was in order to offer sacrifices from them. This reason alone is not sufficient, for not all clean animals are fit for sacrifice — such as the gazelle and the deer — and not every clean bird is fit for sacrifice, so how can one infer from this that Noah learned Torah? Rather, the principal reason is that because they are clean, one eats from them a lot; therefore they need to be multiplied and to reproduce more, for the sake of those who keep the Torah. And concerning what is said (Genesis 8:20), ״וַיִּקַּח מִכֹּל הַבְּהֵמָה הַטְּהוֹרָה״ and so on — this implies that he offered from every clean species, even from those that are not fit for sacrifice according to the Torah. The answer to this matter is that Noah did not yet know that the Holy One, blessed be He, would permit him to eat living creatures, and he said in his heart: surely for this reason He commanded me to bring seven and seven of all these species — in order to offer sacrifices from all of them. But the Holy One, blessed be He, knew that in the end He would permit him all clean living creatures; therefore He commanded to take seven and seven, because one hunts from them a lot. For one cannot say that He wanted the sacrifice of all species, for if so, why did He not permit for all generations to sacrifice from every clean species when they would be available to man, and why did He command specifically Noah? And concerning what is said (Genesis 8:21), ״וַיָּרַח ה׳ אֶת רֵיחַ הַנִּיחֹחַ״ — that was on account of those species fit for sacrifice; and as for the rest, it was merely a savor of roasted meat."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 7, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:27:34.699495476Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}